# Forge Developer Guide — {{ model.names.dot_net_class_name }} (gRPC)

> Generated by Pervaxis Forge for the **{{ model.manifest.product }}** vertical.
> This guide is your primary reference for writing vertical domain code on this service.

---

## 1. The Contract: What You Own vs What You Don't

### Authentication model
- Internal east-west traffic should use mTLS or mesh identity at the transport layer.
- The scaffold includes API-key auth for compatibility, but that is not the preferred service-to-service identity model.
- If the deployment uses a mesh or service edge, prefer moving identity enforcement there and keep the service authorization policy aligned with that decision.

### HSTS guidance
- The scaffold sets HSTS in non-development environments.
- Keep HSTS when the service terminates TLS directly.
- Treat HSTS as redundant when TLS is terminated upstream by a mesh or edge proxy.

### Browser-only controls
- Do not add CORS as a runtime requirement for a pure gRPC east-west service.
- Do not treat browser security headers as a primary gRPC control surface.
- If the service later exposes browser-facing HTTP endpoints, scope those controls to the HTTP edge instead of the gRPC path.

### You own (vertical code)
- `ServiceImpl.cs` — gRPC service method implementations
- `Protos/` — proto file additions (message types, new RPCs)
- `Data/` — EF Core `DbContext`, entities, repositories, migrations
- `Tests/` — all test cases

### You do NOT touch (horizontal — raise a Pervaxis ticket for changes)
- `Program.cs` — app startup, gRPC wiring, Genesis modules
- `ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs` — DI registration structure
- `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.*` — container configuration
- `.github/workflows/` — CI/CD pipelines

If you find yourself editing a horizontal file, stop and raise a Pervaxis platform ticket.

