## 3. Proto Definition

Add your domain message types and RPC definitions to `Protos/{{ model.manifest.service_name }}.proto`:

```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";

option csharp_namespace = "{{ model.names.namespace }}";

package {{ model.manifest.service_name | string.replace "-" "_" }};

service {{ model.names.dot_net_class_name }}Service {
  rpc Get (GetRequest) returns (RecordResponse);
  rpc Create (CreateRequest) returns (RecordResponse);
  rpc Update (UpdateRequest) returns (RecordResponse);
  rpc Delete (DeleteRequest) returns (DeleteResponse);
  rpc List (ListRequest) returns (stream RecordResponse);
}

message GetRequest {
  string id = 1;
}

message CreateRequest {
  string reference = 1;
  string submitted_by = 2;
  int64 submitted_at_unix = 3;
}

message UpdateRequest {
  string id = 1;
  string status = 2;
  string notes = 3;
}

message DeleteRequest {
  string id = 1;
}

message DeleteResponse {
  bool success = 1;
}

message RecordResponse {
  string id = 1;
  string reference = 2;
  string status = 3;
  int64 created_at_unix = 4;
  int64 updated_at_unix = 5;
}

message ListRequest {
  int32 page = 1;
  int32 page_size = 2;
}
```

**Proto conventions:**
- Use `snake_case` for field names in proto
- Use `int64` for timestamps (Unix milliseconds) — never proto `Timestamp` for Pervaxis services
- Keep proto messages flat — avoid deep nesting

### Proto Versioning Strategy

<!-- [10.2] When to cut v2, backward compatibility, field deprecation -->

**Never break the existing contract.** gRPC / proto3 allows additive changes without a version bump:

| Change | Safe? |
|---|---|
| Add a new field | ✅ Yes — defaults to zero-value on old clients |
| Add a new RPC | ✅ Yes |
| Rename a field (keep the number) | ✅ Yes — wire format uses numbers, not names |
| Remove a field without reserving its number | ⚠️ Risk — number may be reused; always `reserved` |
| Change a field type | ❌ No — break old clients |
| Change a field number | ❌ No — wire-format breaking change |

**When to cut a `v2` package:**
- You need to change a field type or remove required fields.
- The API contract is significantly restructured (not just extended).
- A new package prefix (e.g., `package myservice.v2;`) lets old and new clients coexist.

**Deprecating a field:**

```protobuf
message RecordResponse {
  string id = 1;
  string reference = 2;
  string status = 3;
  int64 created_at_unix = 4;
  int64 updated_at_unix = 5;
  string legacy_code = 6 [deprecated = true];  // kept for wire compat; ignored by new clients
  reserved 7, 8;                               // never reuse these numbers
  reserved "old_field_name";                   // never reuse this name
}
```

### Pagination

<!-- [10.3] Standardized pagination approach -->

Pervaxis gRPC services standardize on **cursor-based pagination** for all `List` RPCs. Offset pagination (`page` + `page_size`) does not scale past a few thousand rows and produces duplicate/missing results when records are inserted during iteration.

```protobuf
// Standard List request — use this pattern for all new List RPCs
message ListRequest {
  // Maximum number of records to return. Server may return fewer.
  int32 page_size = 1;  // max: 100, default: 20

  // Opaque cursor from the previous response's next_page_token.
  // Omit or leave empty to start from the beginning.
  string page_token = 2;
}

message ListResponse {
  repeated RecordResponse records = 1;

  // Opaque cursor for the next page. Empty string means no more pages.
  string next_page_token = 2;

  // Total count is expensive — omit unless the client genuinely needs it
  // (e.g., to render a pagination UI). Omitting it keeps List queries O(page_size).
  // int32 total_count = 3;
}
```

**Server-side cursor implementation:**

```csharp
// Encode the last seen ID as the cursor (base64 for opacity)
var cursor = records.Count == pageSize
    ? Convert.ToBase64String(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(records.Last().Id))
    : string.Empty;

return new ListResponse
{
    Records = { records.Select(r => r.ToResponse()) },
    NextPageToken = cursor
};

// Decode on next call:
var afterId = string.IsNullOrEmpty(request.PageToken)
    ? null
    : Encoding.UTF8.GetString(Convert.FromBase64String(request.PageToken));
```

### Rich Error Model

<!-- [10.4] google.rpc.Status + ErrorInfo for structured error payloads -->

gRPC's basic `Status(StatusCode.X, "message")` is opaque — clients can only read the status code and a string. For errors that clients need to handle programmatically (validation failures, conflict details, rate limiting), use the **gRPC rich error model**.

Add to proto:

```protobuf
import "google/rpc/status.proto";
import "google/rpc/error_details.proto";
```

Throw structured errors from service methods:

```csharp
using Google.Rpc;
using Grpc.Core;

// Validation error — tells the client exactly which fields are wrong
var badRequest = new BadRequest();
badRequest.FieldViolations.Add(new BadRequest.Types.FieldViolation
{
    Field   = "reference",
    Description = "Reference must be between 3 and 100 characters."
});

var richStatus = new Google.Rpc.Status
{
    Code    = (int)Code.InvalidArgument,
    Message = "Validation failed.",
    Details = { Any.Pack(badRequest) }
};

throw richStatus.ToRpcException();

// Resource not found with debug info:
var notFound = new ResourceInfo
{
    ResourceType = "YourDomainRecord",
    ResourceName = request.Id,
    Description  = "Record does not exist or you do not have access."
};
throw new Google.Rpc.Status
{
    Code    = (int)Code.NotFound,
    Message = "Resource not found.",
    Details = { Any.Pack(notFound) }
}.ToRpcException();
```

Add the NuGet package to the csproj (already in `Directory.Packages.props` for future versions):

```xml
<PackageReference Include="Google.Api.CommonProtos" />
```

