```csharp
// Entity and DbContext
public sealed class YourDomainEntity
{
    public required string Id { get; set; }
    public required string Reference { get; set; }
    public required string Status { get; set; }
    public required DateTimeOffset CreatedAt { get; set; }
}

public sealed class YourDomainDbContext : DbContext
{
    public YourDomainDbContext(DbContextOptions<YourDomainDbContext> options) : base(options) { }
    public DbSet<YourDomainEntity> Records => Set<YourDomainEntity>();

    protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
    {
        modelBuilder.Entity<YourDomainEntity>(entity =>
        {
            entity.HasKey(e => e.Id);
            entity.Property(e => e.Reference).HasMaxLength(100).IsRequired();
            entity.HasIndex(e => e.Reference).IsUnique();
        });
    }
}
```

### Connection Pooling

<!-- [3.2.2] Configure Npgsql connection pool for high-throughput gRPC services -->

Npgsql maintains a connection pool per connection string. Tune these via the connection string or `NpgsqlDataSourceBuilder`:

```csharp
// In Program.cs or NpgsqlDataSourceBuilder configuration:
builder.Services.AddDbContextPool<YourDomainDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseNpgsql(
        connectionString,
        npgsql =>
        {
            npgsql.EnableRetryOnFailure(3);
            // Connection Lifetime: recycle connections periodically to avoid stale TCP
            // sessions after network topology changes. 300s is a safe default.
            // Tune MinPoolSize / MaxPoolSize in the connection string:
            // "...;Minimum Pool Size=2;Maximum Pool Size=50;Connection Lifetime=300"
        }));
```

**Connection string parameters (recommended starting point):**

| Parameter | Default | Guidance |
|---|---|---|
| `Minimum Pool Size` | 0 | Set to 2–5 to keep warm connections ready under low traffic |
| `Maximum Pool Size` | 100 | For gRPC, start at 20–50; monitor `npgsql.pool.connections` metric |
| `Connection Lifetime` | 0 (unlimited) | Set 300–600 seconds to prevent stale connections after failover |
| `Connection Idle Lifetime` | 300 | Lower under low traffic to free server-side connections |
| `Keepalive` | 0 (disabled) | Set 30 to prevent firewall/LB idle-connection drops |

### Soft Delete

<!-- [3.2.4] Global query filter for soft-delete pattern -->

If entities use an `IsDeleted` flag, set a global query filter in `OnModelCreating` so queries automatically exclude deleted records. **Without this, every LINQ query must explicitly filter `IsDeleted == false`.**

```csharp
public interface ISoftDeletable
{
    bool IsDeleted { get; set; }
    DateTimeOffset? DeletedAt { get; set; }
}

// In OnModelCreating — apply globally so no query ever sees deleted rows by default
protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
{
    base.OnModelCreating(modelBuilder);
    modelBuilder.HasDefaultSchema("{{ model.names.database_schema }}");

    foreach (var entityType in modelBuilder.Model.GetEntityTypes())
    {
        if (typeof(ISoftDeletable).IsAssignableFrom(entityType.ClrType))
        {
            var parameter = Expression.Parameter(entityType.ClrType, "e");
            var body = Expression.Equal(
                Expression.Property(parameter, nameof(ISoftDeletable.IsDeleted)),
                Expression.Constant(false));
            modelBuilder.Entity(entityType.ClrType).HasQueryFilter(Expression.Lambda(body, parameter));
        }
    }
}
```

To query deleted records when needed: `_dbContext.Records.IgnoreQueryFilters().Where(...)`.

### Migrations

```powershell
dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate --project src/{{ model.names.namespace }}
dotnet ef database update --project src/{{ model.names.namespace }}
```

### Migrations in CI/CD

<!-- [11.5] Guidance on applying migrations in production deployment pipelines -->

**Do not call `database update` inside the application on startup** — it creates race conditions when multiple replicas start simultaneously.

**Recommended pattern: pre-deploy migration job**

```yaml
# In your deployment pipeline (GitHub Actions / AWS CodePipeline):

# Option A — ECS task (run-before-deploy):
- name: Apply migrations
  run: |
    dotnet ef database update \
      --project src/{{ model.names.namespace }} \
      --connection "${{ secrets.DB_CONNECTION_STRING }}"

# Option B — Kubernetes init container:
# Add to the pod spec:
#   initContainers:
#   - name: migrate
#     image: <your-service-image>
#     command: ["dotnet", "ef", "database", "update"]
#     env:
#     - name: ConnectionStrings__Default
#       valueFrom:
#         secretKeyRef:
#           name: db-secret
#           key: connection-string
```

**Migration checklist before deploying:**
1. Migrations must be idempotent (EF Core handles this via the `__EFMigrationsHistory` table).
2. Additive migrations (add column, add table) are safe for rolling deploys.
3. Destructive migrations (drop column, rename) require a two-phase deploy: deprecate → deploy v1 → remove → deploy v2.
4. Add a manual approval gate in your pipeline before applying destructive migrations in production.
5. Always apply migrations in a transaction — Npgsql wraps DDL by default for PostgreSQL.

